---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
import { loadIndex } from '../lib/catalog';

let index;
try { index = loadIndex(); } catch { index = null; }
const sample = index?.images[0];
const ownerRepo = index ? `${index.owner}/${index.repo}` : 'OWNER/REPO';
const latestTag = index?.latestTag ?? 'vX.Y.Z';

// Build a real, valid pull-ref for the example. OCI repository names are
// lowercase only; the catalog already stores the lowercased form so we use
// that rather than reconstructing from (language, version, tier).
const sampleImageName = sample
  ? `clearcutt-${sample.language}${sample.languageVersion}`.toLowerCase()
  : 'clearcutt-corelts';
const sampleRegistryBase = index?.registryBase ?? `ghcr.io/${ownerRepo.toLowerCase()}`;
const sampleRefVersioned = `${sampleRegistryBase}/${sampleImageName}:${latestTag}-${sample?.tier ?? 'slim'}`;

// The cert SAN baked in by GitHub Actions OIDC is the exact workflow file URL
// at the ref that built the image. Pin the same regex for BOTH the signature
// and the SBOM attestation so an attacker who somehow got a different workflow
// signed in this repo can't satisfy the check.
const workflowIdentityRegex =
  `^https://github\\.com/${ownerRepo}/\\.github/workflows/tag-release\\.yml@refs/tags/v.*$`;
const oidcIssuer = 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com';
---
<Base title="Verification guide">
  <article class="space-y-10 max-w-3xl">
    <header class="space-y-3">
      <h1 class="heading text-3xl">Verifying ClearCutt images</h1>
      <p class="text-ink-200">
        Every ClearCutt image carries independent attestations stored alongside the manifest in
        GHCR: a Sigstore signature (keyless OIDC), an SPDX SBOM, and SLSA Level 3 provenance from
        the official slsa-github-generator — plus a GitHub-native attestation of the same
        provenance and SBOM. (GHCR doesn't expose the OCI 1.1 Referrers API, so these are kept
        under the referrers tag schema against each image digest; cosign v3's new bundle format
        consolidates them.) You verify with whichever standard upstream tool you already trust;
        they validate the same signed material, so no single vendor is load-bearing.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-300">
        The examples below use <code class="font-mono text-ink-100">{sampleImageName}</code>
        pinned to <code class="font-mono text-ink-100">{latestTag}</code>. Swap in any image
        from the catalog — the verification flow is identical.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">1. Install tools</h2>
      <CopyBlock language="shell" code={`# macOS / Linux\nbrew install cosign slsa-verifier crane jq gh`} />
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-300">
        Pinned versions in CI: cosign v3.0+, slsa-verifier v2.7+, crane v0.20+. The
        <code class="font-mono text-ink-100">gh</code> CLI (step 5) is optional — use it if you
        prefer GitHub's attestation store over running cosign directly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">2. Verify the signature</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-200">
        ClearCutt uses keyless OIDC — there's no static public key. Instead you assert which
        workflow file built the image (the cert SAN) and which OIDC issuer signed the cert.
        The regex below pins both pieces literally; everything except the tag suffix is fixed.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock language="shell" code={`cosign verify ${sampleRefVersioned} \\\n  --certificate-identity-regexp '${workflowIdentityRegex}' \\\n  --certificate-oidc-issuer '${oidcIssuer}'`} />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">3. Verify the SBOM attestation</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-200">
        Confirms the SPDX SBOM on the manifest was produced by the same workflow run that
        signed the image. Same identity regex as step 2 — never loosen it (matching any
        workflow under the repo would let any reusable workflow signed in this repo satisfy
        the check, which defeats the point).
      </p>
      <CopyBlock language="shell" code={`cosign verify-attestation ${sampleRefVersioned} \\\n  --type spdxjson \\\n  --certificate-identity-regexp '${workflowIdentityRegex}' \\\n  --certificate-oidc-issuer '${oidcIssuer}' \\\n  | jq '.payload | @base64d | fromjson | .predicate'`} />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">4. Verify SLSA L3 provenance</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-200">
        Confirms the build was non-falsifiable — produced by the upstream
        slsa-github-generator running on hosted GitHub runners, from the exact source tag.
        The source-tag must match the image tag.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock language="shell" code={`# Resolve the multi-arch digest first\nDIGEST=$(crane digest ${sampleRefVersioned})\nslsa-verifier verify-image ${sampleRegistryBase}/${sampleImageName}@$DIGEST \\\n  --source-uri 'github.com/${ownerRepo}' \\\n  --source-tag '${latestTag}'`} />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">5. Verify with the GitHub CLI (alternative to cosign)</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-200">
        The same build provenance and SBOM are also published as GitHub-native attestations.
        If you'd rather not run cosign, <code class="font-mono text-ink-100">gh attestation
        verify</code> checks them against GitHub's attestation store and the Sigstore
        public-good transparency log — pinning the owner so only attestations produced by this
        org's workflows are trusted.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock language="shell" code={`# Resolve the digest, then verify the GitHub-native attestation\nDIGEST=$(crane digest ${sampleRefVersioned})\ngh attestation verify oci://${sampleRegistryBase}/${sampleImageName}@$DIGEST \\\n  --owner ${index?.owner ?? 'OWNER'}`} />
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <h2 class="heading text-xl">6. Enforce at admission</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-200">
        For Kubernetes, every image detail page exposes a copy-pasteable Kyverno
        <code class="font-mono text-ink-100">ClusterPolicy</code> that wires the same
        certificate identity and issuer into a cluster-side admission gate. The policy uses
        a literal subject (not a regex) for an exact pin.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
